The sequence $x_n$ converges to $c$ in $\ell_1$, meaning $\|x_n - c\|_1 \to 0$. For any $p > 1$, we use the inequality $\|x_n - c\|_p \leq \|x_n - c\|_1$, which holds because each term $|x_n(k) - c(k)|^p \leq |x_n(k) - c(k)|$ when $|x_n(k) - c(k)| \leq 1$ (since the $\ell_1$ norm is finite, terms must eventually be small). Thus, the $\ell_p$ norm of the difference also tends to zero. 

**Final Answer**
\boxed{True}

